A||||12||SLYGUY||Limp pedal.....||||8||
Z||000000||SLYGUY||10-16-2001||03:49 AM||||Here goes:<BR>The car wasn't easy to get into first gear from the get go. Once the car warmed up, it got a little easier.<P>Tonight, I had to have the clutch buried into the carpet to get the car into first. <BR>I figured that I needed to bleed the lines. <BR>I found the bleed valve. It had a cap that refused to come off. Had to rip it off.<BR>Had an assistant push the clutch pedal to the floor. I cracked the bleeder counter clockwise and some fluid squirted out.<BR>Good so far right...<BR>Then closed the valve careful not to turn it too far. Had the assistant pump the clutch pedal (is this right?) and hold it to the floor. Opened the valved, less fluid squirted out. Checked the level in the master cyclinder and it had dropped a little. Topped it off. <P>Now the clutch pedal is a lifeless useless item. What did I do wrong?<BR>I think I remember hearing something about having the rear of the car on jackstands. I also read about it after I searched for "Bleeding Clutch".<BR>Did having the car on all four wheels pump the system full of air?<BR>HELP!!!!<P>Sly<P>||||reg||8
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||10-16-2001||09:25 AM||||Sounds like a lot of air in the system now. A common mistake made by a lot of people who bleed their clutch system is by pumping the pedal. This is to be avoided always.<BR>A couple of ?'s for you, how old is the slave cylinder and what color was the fluid that came out of it?<BR>From your description of the removing the cover from the bleeder it sounds as if it had not been off in a long time. If the fluid is dark and almost black the risk of rusted components is certain. I would bet that your slave cylinder is toast or there is a small piece of dirt (rust) under the seat of the bleeder valve. Try bleeding it again after removing the bleeder and make sure that there isn't anything blocking the valves seat.||||reg||1
Z||000002||SLYGUY||10-16-2001||12:21 PM||||bump*||||reg||1
Z||000003||dman||10-16-2001||03:12 PM||||Hello slyguy.<P>Indiana_resto_guy offers some good advice. I'd suggest you try it as well as give us more feedback rather than posting a one line "bump" reply. Doing so gives us no idea as to whether or not the previous advice worked, as well as preventing us from giving your further, more refined and accurate advice because we have no additional information to work off of. We're not psychics(sp?) you know.<P>Did you try bleeding it again? You should definitely get fluid coming out of the bleeder when you crack it open. In your description it sounds odd that on your second attempt "Opened the valved, less fluid squirted out"<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||SLYGUY||10-16-2001||04:33 PM||||I'm sorry, I only hit bump when I checked my post this morning and there were no replies. <P>The fluid in the reservoir was dark, like the cap had never been off and the fluid had never been changed. <BR>I bought a Mytyvac pum system with all of the fun little fittings and hoses as well as a quart of brakefluid. I used the vac to siphon all of the fluid out of the reservoir and when I got to the bottom there was a lot of gunk I had to scrape out. I didn't see anything that would lead me to believe that there is rust in the system, just looked like the cap went on at the factory and never removed again.<BR>I refilled the reservoir with fresh brake fluid and have been applying pressure to the fitting with the vacuum, cracking it open and getting lots of bubbles, closing the fitting and repeating.<BR>There is so much air that now I'm thinking that I must have a leak or something. <BR>There was no leak before, you just had to have the clutch to the floor to get the car to shift. I figured that I needed to bleed the system and got myself into one heck of a mess!!<BR>I really do appreciate all of your guys' help. I always look here first.<P>Sly<P>P.S. I didn't get a response, that is why I replied with a *bump. Promise<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||10-16-2001||04:43 PM||||I believe V-8 Archie has a procedure in his website for bleeding the clutch.  Not sure though, I can't afford a v-8 swap now and it hurts to look.<P>Maybe some day Archie.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Patrick86GT||10-16-2001||06:08 PM||||Carrolles, right you are!<P>SLY, go read this thru and do it this way.<BR> <A HREF="http://home.v8archie.com/archism.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://home.v8archie.com/archism.htm</A> <P>If bleeding doesn't do it, there are a number of items to review: leaks, pedal travel (possible bent unit), master and/or slave cylinder blow-by, bent fork, and clutch disc assy.<P>In my experience, the symptom of grinding into 1st gear is evidence that the clutch disc is wearing thin. Bleeding can buy some time (miles), but the inevitable is replacement of the clutch, pressure plate and bearing (and maybe the fork).<P>Good Luck!||||reg||1
Z||000007||SLYGUY||10-16-2001||09:40 PM||||I had a problem with it grinding only when I forced the issue. <P>Turned out that my master cylinder was shot. Bleeding it was a lost cause. I then proceeded to do something really STUPID. <P>I went to Autozone with my core and bought a remanufactured. They obviously think that you are an idiot to buy something that failed for the same reason(s) that yours did after they apparently only ran the rebuild through a pressure washer, put it in a bag, and put a price tag on it. The rebuild was every bit as shot in all the same ways that mine was. <BR>I have read many many times that you should NEVER buy a rebuilt one because even if you are successful in bleeding it (very big IF) it will not last long. I was unable to bleed it, and it would not consume fluid from its own reservoir. I bought a Mityvac to assist in bleeding it and it came with a small container. I improvised and used this container as an surrogate master cylinder reservoir and was able to bleed the system all the way back to the tranny and have it full of fluid. You would get plenty of pressure at the pedal, but it was just air. As soon as you tried to bleed either the master cylinder or the bleeder valve you would wind up in the same condition. Took the rebuilt master cylinder out and it worked every bit the same way as the one I had earlier that was shot. It would NOT use it's own reservoir. <P>I took it back and got my money back, the only good thing that happened all day. I called the local Pontiac dealer and they can order me one. It will take a couple of days, but is only $40 bucks more than the "rebuilt". <BR>I have read how much easier it is to bleed a genuine new GM clutch master cylinder, and have a printout of Archie's way that is much more informative and helpful. <P>I'll keep you guys posted. Wish me luck.<BR>Again, thank you for all of your help as usual!! You guys ROCK!!<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfiero||10-16-2001||09:59 PM||||I'm a big proponent of using NEW GM clutch hydraulics.  The way I look at it is the original one lasted this long, so another new one should do the same (10+ years).  <P>Then there is the fact that I have already tried using the aftermarket replacements with little success.  Everyone wants to save a few bucks whenever they can, but this isn't the way to go.  It is worth it in the long run to spend that extra $40 or so than to be constantly replacing or bleeding the POS aftermarket junk all the time.<P>Let us know how it goes when you get the new one installed.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||SLYGUY||10-17-2001||03:29 AM||||I didn't get a chance to order it today. My buddy and me were so angry about that POS rebuild that we tore it all apart. Found an orifice that was plugged. <BR>We weren't about to go through the trouble or rebuilding a rebuilt, but we just had to know. <BR>I won't ever go rebuilt again. You are already saving tons of money doing the labor yourself, put it towards quality parts. <BR>If it turns out that the slave wants to die at the same time, I'll get that OEM also. I should get it by the weekend and I'll let you guys know.<P>Sly<P>P.S. I wonder how long that, gulp, rebuilt alternator is gonna last me. $109 mistake?<BR>Damn<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||TooManyFieros||10-18-2001||02:32 PM||||Don't forget, when using MIGHTYVAC, remove the bleeder screw and put tefflon tape on the threads. Otherwise, you will suck a little air in around the threads. It will appear that you have air in the system when you don't. Also, pull the slave piston into the slave cylinder with the bleed screw way open (slowly pull on the clutch shaft arm and don't release till the bleeder screw is tight. As above, see Archie's page on clutch bleeding. Works well. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Joe Torma||10-18-2001||07:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TooManyFieros:<BR><B>Don't forget, when using MIGHTYVAC, remove the bleeder screw and put tefflon tape on the threads</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That should be the tip of the day.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I pretty much spent an hour trying the mityvac(supposed to be easier, right) yesterday.||||reg||1
Z||000012||SLYGUY||10-18-2001||11:23 PM||||I guess that is why I spent between 1-2 hours bleeding the thing. I had a great pedal tension within 15 minutes, but I kept getting air in the vacuum tube. I'd pump it up, crack open the bleeder, many bubbles would run up along with some fluid, I'd close the bleeder just before the guage bottomed out at zero, and repeat. <P>That idea with the Teflon tape sounds like a winner. You learn SO much with this board. <P>Clutch works better than ever. <BR>Thanks again guys   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Sly||||reg||11
